   p(3) &= 3^2 - (4-\sqrt{11})3 + (4-\sqrt{11})^2q(3) \\
   &= 9 - 12\sqrt{11} + 25q(3) \\
   p(4) &= 4^2 - (4-\sqrt{11})4 + (4-\sqrt{11})^2q(4) \\
   &= 16 - 16\sqrt{11} + 25q(4)
   \end{align*}

5. To find $q(3)$ and $q(4)$, we can substitute $x=3$ and $x=4$ into the equation $q(x) = x^2 - 8x + 17$:
   \begin{align*}
   q(3) &= 3^2 - 8\cdot 3 + 17 = 12 \\
   q(4) &= 4^2 - 8\cdot 4 + 17 = 9
   \end{align*}

6. Substituting these values back into the expressions for $p(3)$ and $p(4)$, we get:
   \begin{align*}
   p(3) &= 9 - 12\sqrt{11} + 25\cdot 12 = 135 - 12\sqrt{11} \\
   p(4) &= 16 - 16\sqrt{11} + 25\cdot 9 = 135 - 16\sqrt{11}
   \end{align*}

7. Finally, we can compute the ratio $\frac{p(3)}{p(4)}$:
   \begin{align*}
   \frac{p(3)}{p(4)} &= \frac{135 - 12\sqrt{11}}{135 - 16\sqrt{11}} \\
   &= 1 - \frac{4\sqrt{11}}{135 - 16\sqrt{11}}
   \end{align*}

The final answer is \boxed{$1 - \frac{4\sqrt{11}}{135 - 16\sqrt{11}}$}.

