First, recall the definition of the radical conjugate:

\begin{align*}
\text{Radical conjugate of } a + b\sqrt{c} &= a - b\sqrt{c} \\
\text{where } a, b, c \text{ are real numbers and } c > 0.
\end{align*}

In our case, $a = 5$ and $b = 1$, $c = 3$. So the radical conjugate of $5+\sqrt{3}$ is $5-\sqrt{3}$.

Now, to compute the product of a number and its radical conjugate, we can use the following property:

\begin{align*}
(a + b\sqrt{c})(a - b\sqrt{c}) &= a^2 - (b\sqrt{c})(a - b\sqrt{c}) \\
&= a^2 - ab\sqrt{c} + ab\sqrt{c} - b^2c \\
&= a^2 - b^2c \\
\end{align*}

Plugging in our values, we get:

\begin{align*}
(5+\sqrt{3})(5-\sqrt{3}) &= 5^2 - 1^2\cdot 3 \\
&= 25 - 3 \\
&= \boxed{22}.
\end{align*}

